<DocsLayout title="Architecture">
  <h1>Architecture</h1>

  <h2>Service shape</h2>
  <p>Identity is a single Go binary. It runs two HTTP servers:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Connect server (port 80 by default)</strong> &mdash; the public API. Connect-RPC over HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2, speaking JSON or proto-binary.</li>
    <li><strong>Metrics server (port 9090 by default)</strong> &mdash; <code>/metrics</code> for Prometheus.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The Connect server also serves three side-channel paths handled before the RPC router:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Path</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/</code>, <code>/health</code>, <code>/healthz</code></td><td>Liveness probe (<code>&#123;"status":"ok"&#125;</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code></td><td>JWKS for downstream services</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Request pipeline</h2>
  <p>Requests pass through this middleware chain (outermost first):</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Logging</strong> &mdash; structured zap log per request</li>
    <li><strong>CORS</strong> &mdash; configured via <code>GATEWAY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS</code></li>
    <li><strong>Health</strong> &mdash; short-circuits <code>/health</code>, <code>/healthz</code>, <code>/</code></li>
    <li><strong>JWKS</strong> &mdash; serves <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code></li>
    <li><strong>Auth</strong> &mdash; bearer-token verification on non-exempt paths</li>
    <li><strong>Connect handler</strong> &mdash; routes to the IdentityService methods</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Auth-exempt paths</h2>
  <p>The auth middleware allows unauthenticated access to login, signup, and other public flows. From <code>internal/middleware/auth.go</code>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>OAuthLogin</code>, <code>PasswordLogin</code>, <code>PasswordSignup</code></li>
    <li><code>RefreshToken</code>, <code>Logout</code>, <code>GetCurrentUser</code></li>
    <li><code>BeginPasskeyLogin</code>, <code>CompletePasskeyLogin</code></li>
    <li><code>InitiateQrLogin</code>, <code>PollQrLogin</code></li>
    <li><code>AcceptInvitation</code>, <code>RequestAdminHelp</code>, <code>VerifyTotp</code></li>
    <li><code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code>, <code>/health</code>, <code>/healthz</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>For exempt paths, if a token is present and valid, the middleware still extracts the <code>sub</code> claim into the <code>X-Authenticated-User-Id</code> request header so the handler can act on the caller identity (used for <code>GetCurrentUser</code>).</p>

  <h2>Service layer</h2>
  <p>The Connect handler is a thin shell. All business logic lives in <code>internal/service/</code>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>AuthService</code> &mdash; signup, login, refresh, logout, OAuth, passkey, TOTP, QR login</li>
    <li><code>AdminService</code> &mdash; invite, deactivate, reactivate, admin password reset, set quota</li>
    <li><code>GroupService</code> &mdash; group CRUD and membership</li>
    <li><code>HelpService</code> &mdash; admin help requests (replaces self-serve forgot-password)</li>
    <li><code>ProfileService</code> &mdash; self-service profile and password changes</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each service takes a <code>Repository</code> or <code>DB</code> interface so it's testable without a live EntDB connection. Errors are domain-typed (e.g. <code>ErrInvalidArgument</code>, <code>ErrAlreadyExists</code>) and translated to gRPC codes by the handler layer.</p>

  <h2>Persistence</h2>
  <p>Every persistent operation goes through EntDB. Identity reserves type IDs <code>1&ndash;99</code>:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>type_id</th><th>Node</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>User</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Group (the proto name; the EntDB node type is still <code>WorkingGroup</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>RefreshToken</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td>Passkey</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td>TOTPSecret</td></tr>
    <tr><td>8</td><td>AuditEvent</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Group membership is an EntDB edge (<code>User &larr;MEMBER_OF&rarr; Group</code>).</p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Why EntDB?">
    Tenant isolation is the load-bearing feature. Each product gets its own tenant; user data is stored in a per-tenant SQLite file under EntDB, with cryptographic shred-by-key support. Identity itself is stateless &mdash; it's safe to run multiple replicas behind a load balancer.
  </Callout>

  <h2>External dependencies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>EntDB</strong> &mdash; required, gRPC at <code>GATEWAY_ENTDB_ADDRESS</code></li>
    <li><strong>Email service</strong> &mdash; optional, gRPC at <code>GATEWAY_EMAIL_SERVICE_HOST:GATEWAY_EMAIL_SERVICE_PORT</code> for sending password-reset and invitation emails</li>
    <li><strong>Google / Microsoft</strong> &mdash; optional, only needed if OAuth is enabled</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Stateless replicas</h2>
  <p>Identity holds no in-memory state that must survive across instances. JWT signing keys come from an env var (the JSON key ring); refresh tokens, sessions, TOTP secrets, and challenges all live in EntDB. You can horizontally scale by running multiple replicas with the same <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS</code>; downstream services don't care which replica issued a token because verification is local via JWKS.</p>
</DocsLayout>
